On Jun 4, 2007, at 11:05 AM, lei.wu at jax.org wrote:

> Thanks, Simon. My boss would like to see the periodic prompt print  
> out from running R codes. Our R code takes really long time to run  
> (1 or 2 days). It basically run thousands iterations. After each  
> iteration, R code prints out a message to let user know the  
> progress. My old Java code with Rserve cannot print these messages  
> out. Since I'm recoding to replace Rserve with JRI, he wants to let  
> the users see these messages to let them know the progress. I  
> played around with JRI's example codes, rtest and rtest2, but  
> cannot get this done. Is it possible? So this is a kind of partial  
> REPL that using Rengine as a computation engine and also want to  
> show the prompt print out from running R program.
>

Sure. Actually, rtest.java does exactly that as-is (it just prints it  
to the console). All you really have to implement is rWriteConsole -  
it will be called whenever R prints something. You can make it to e- 
mail the intermediate result to your boss or whatever you like ;). If  
you have problems with that, please be more specific as you what your  
code does, because the rtest.java example actually does exactly what  
you describe.


> If I initialize Rengine re = new Rengine(args, true, new  
> TextConsole2()), do I have to input R command from keyboard?
>

You don't have to, but you can. Clearly, you could change  
rReadConsole such that it doesn't read from the stdin and add your  
queue instead if you want a true console. But there is no real  
difference in just using eval (because that's all the REPL does).

Cheers,
Simon



